==Trivia==
*This is the first season to have a grand prize of $750,000 as opposed to $500,000, and the first season in which the grand prize is guaranteed to be greater than $500,000.
**In addition, for the first time ever, the runner-up consolation prize also increased from $50,000 to $75,000, while the [[America's Favorite Houseguest]] prize increased from $25,000 to $50,000.
**In ''[[Big Brother 6 (US)]]'', if both members of a secret pair made it to the Final Two together their prize money increased, meaning that the winner would receive $1,000,000 and the runner-up $250,000.<ref>https://www.realityblurred.com/realitytv/2005/06/big-brother-6-secrets/</ref> That did not happen, so the eventual winner and runner-up, [[Maggie Ausburn]] and [[Ivette Corredero]], only received the usual $500,000 and $50,000 respectively.
*This season is tied with ''[[Big Brother 22 (US)]]'' for the longest time before a woman won an HOH or a POV, with the first HOH or POV win by a female occurring on Week 5.
*This is the first season where men won the first six HOHs.
*This is the first season in which no white men made it to the jury phase. All four of them ([[Christian Birkenberger|Christian]], [[Brent Champagne|Brent]], [[Frenchie French|Frenchie]] and [[Travis Long|Travis]]) were evicted during the pre-jury phase.
*BB23 holds the record for the largest amount of female jurors in Big Brother history, with 7 female jurors.
*This is the eighth season to have females win 4 HOH's in a row following [[Big Brother 5]], [[Big Brother 8]], [[Big Brother 10]], [[Big Brother 10]], [[Big Brother 13]], [[Big Brother 17]], [[Big Brother: Over The Top]], and [[Big Brother 20]].
*This is the first season to enact the CBS diversity initiative where at least 50% of the cast was BIPOC.
*With [[The Cookout]] making it to the Final 6 on Day 65, this season became the first to have an entirely African-American Final 6, and thus became the American civilian season to have an African-American winner, a title which eventually went to [[Xavier Prather]].
**The Cookout making it to Final 6 also marks the first time an alliance of at least four people forms within Week 1 and successfully evicts every other houseguest not in the alliance before losing their first member.
**Although brief due to the [[Double Eviction]], also on Day 65, the Final 7 was made up of all minorities for the first time in ''Big Brother'' history ([[Azah Awasum|the]] [[Derek Frazier|six]] [[Tiffany Mitchell|African]]-[[Xavier Prather|Americans]] who formed the Cookout, including two mixed race members [[Hannah Chaddha|one of Indian descent]] and [[Kyland Young|the other of Mexican descent]]) alongside [[Alyssa Lopez|a woman of Puerto Rican descent]].
*This is the first season since [[Big Brother 18 (US)|Big Brother 18]] where no Houseguest won more than 3 PoV competitions.
*While not confirmed, it is heavily rumored that this season was set to have a [[Triple Eviction]] on Day 71, but production changed the schedule to have two Double Evictions back to back on Days 65 and 71, in order for The Cookout to have a chance at being in the house by themselves for a full week if they managed to become the Final 6. The rumor has credibility with the Day 71 eviction episode being scheduled for two hours, which was the same episode length as Season 22's Triple Eviction.<ref>https://screenrant.com/big-brother-23-triple-eviction-fears-fan-reaction/</ref><ref>https://bigbrothernetwork.com/big-brother-23-triple-eviction-looms-september-two-hour-episode/</ref><ref>https://x.com/BillyGiese/status/1428208041686614022/</ref>

